# Copy to .env on the VM (never commit .env itself — see apps/README.md).
# chmod 600 it: main is force-mirrored to a public repo on every push, and
# these are the only three lines of edge config that live outside git.
#
# All three are IDENTIFIERS, not secrets — there is no credential in this file.
# Caddy authenticates to Azure DNS as one of the VM's attached user-assigned
# managed identities (homelab-edge-dns-identity, infra/identity), never a client
# secret.

AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID=
AZURE_DNS_RESOURCE_GROUP=homelab-rg

# REQUIRED since E15.4 (#100). It used to be an optional fallback, commented out,
# on the grounds that the VM carried exactly one managed identity so selection
# could not be ambiguous. It now carries TWO — this one and
# homelab-backup-identity, which restic authenticates as — and
# DefaultAzureCredential cannot choose between them on its own.
#
# Read this next part before deciding to leave it blank. The failure is NOT at
# deploy time: Caddy starts fine, serves the certificate it already has from
# /data/caddy, and looks completely healthy. It breaks weeks later, at renewal,
# when the DNS-01 challenge cannot get a token for the right identity — by which
# point the change that caused it is long merged. That is why this is pinned in
# the same PR that attached the second identity, rather than left to be
# discovered.
#
# The value is homelab-edge-dns-identity's client id — NOT the backup identity's:
#   terraform -chdir=infra/identity output -raw edge_dns_client_id
#
# Still do NOT set AZURE_TENANT_ID alongside it. A partial credential triple
# (tenant + client, no secret) makes EnvironmentCredential the first thing
# DefaultAzureCredential tries, and it fails differently than it would have.
AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
